{"page": 47, "text": "Sales and marketing expenses increased by $88.6 million, or 15%, and $349.8 million, or 21%, for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, respectively. The increase for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026 was primarily driven by higher Cash App peer-to-peer processing costs and related transaction losses of $37.5 million and $168.5 million, respectively, higher marketing and advertising costs of $20.1 million and $69.4 million, respectively, as we prioritize marketing investments to support the growth of Cash App and Square, and higher personnel costs of $29.5 million and $58.1 million, respectively. The higher personnel costs primarily reflect higher sales commissions as we continue to scale our Square field sales team, and, for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, severance and related expenses recognized in the first quarter of 2026 in connection with the Workforce Plan.\nGeneral and administrative expenses increased by $303.5 million, or 56%, and $1.0 billion, or 70%, for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, respectively. The increase for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026 was primarily driven by an increase in accrued expenses for estimated and settled amounts in connection with certain litigation and regulatory matters of $421.1 million and $969.2 million, respectively, and a net increase in allocated IT, human resources, and facilities expenses of $18.3 million and $124.7 million, respectively, reflecting higher IT shared-service costs allocated to general and administrative expenses and lower human resources costs allocated to other functions following the Workforce Plan. These increases were partially offset by a decrease in personnel costs of $130.2 million and $138.3 million, respectively, driven by an in-person company event held in the third quarter of 2025 as well as cost efficiencies realized following the Workforce Plan actions in the first quarter of 2026.\nTransaction, loan, and consumer receivable losses increased by $238.3 million, or 66%, and $860.0 million, or 104%, for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, respectively, as detailed below (in thousands, except for percentages):\nThree Months Ended\nSeptember 30, Nine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n2026 2025 $ Change % Change 2026 2025 $ Change % Change\nLoan losses $ 439,008 $ 239,689 $ 199,319 83 % $ 1,241,367 $ 463,573 $ 777,794 168 %\nConsumer receivable losses (i) 98,954 77,956 20,998 27 % 260,801 210,493 50,308 24 %\nTransaction losses 63,744 45,810 17,934 39 % 185,113 153,168 31,945 21 %\nTotal transaction, loan, and consumer receivable losses $ 601,706 $ 363,455 $ 238,251 66 % $ 1,687,281 $ 827,234 $ 860,047 104 %\n"}
{"page": 48, "text": "(i) Amounts do not include reserves for certain receivables, such as late fees. Consumer receivables losses also include provision for charge-back losses that are realized and written-off within the same period, rather than through the allowance for consumer receivable losses.\n\u2022Loan losses increased by $199.3 million, or 83%, and $777.8 million, or 168%, compared to the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, respectively. The increase in loan losses was driven by significant growth in loan origination volumes of Cash App Borrow, the continued scaling of Afterpay Post-Purchase and Afterpay Pre-Purchase, as well as growth in Afterpay Pay Monthly and Square Loans. Underlying loan loss rates and portfolio credit performance remained stable. Additionally, beginning in the second quarter of 2025, Cash App Borrow and certain other loan products were retained on our balance sheet and classified as held for investment, for which we recognize expected credit losses upfront at origination. As a result, loan losses in a given period reflect the volume, mix, and timing of originations and underlying credit performance.\n\u2022Consumer receivable losses increased by $21.0 million, or 27%, and $50.3 million, or 24%, compared to the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, respectively, while loss rates remained stable. The increase was primarily due to growth of our Pay in 4 BNPL product and higher origination volumes from preceding periods that reached charge-off during the current quarter.\n\u2022Transaction losses increased by $17.9 million, or 39%, and $31.9 million, or 21%, compared to the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, respectively. The increase was primarily due to higher Square chargeback losses and higher Cash App Card dispute losses, driven by growth in Square GPV and Cash App Card transactions.\n45\nInterest Expense (Income), Net (in thousands, except for percentages)\nThree Months Ended\nSeptember 30, Nine Mo